<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio Gramsci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Antonio Francesco Gramsci</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio Gramsci was an Italian Marxist philosopher, journalist and politician, a founding member and one-time leader of the Italian Communist Party. Born in Ales on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, he became a vocal critic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> and fascism, and was imprisoned in 1926, remaining in prison until shortly before his death in 1937. There he wrote more than 30 notebooks and 3,000 pages of history and analysis, the Prison Notebooks, on subjects from the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to fascism, Fordism and popular culture. He is best known for his theory of cultural hegemony, which describes how the ruling capitalist class uses cultural institutions and ideology, rather than violence or coercion, to keep its power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-gramsci.jpg" alt="Antonio Gramsci" width="330" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gramsci.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gramsci was born in Ales, in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oristano">Oristano</a>, the fourth of seven sons of Francesco Gramsci and Giuseppina Marcias. His father was a low-level official of Arbëreshë (Italo-Albanian) descent, and the family moved through several Sardinian villages before settling in Ghilarza. In 1898 Francesco was convicted of embezzlement and imprisoned, which left the family destitute. The young Gramsci had to leave school and take casual jobs until his father's release in 1904.</p><p>In 1911 he won a scholarship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-turin">University of Turin</a>, sitting the examination at the same time as Palmiro Togliatti. He studied literature and linguistics under Matteo Bartoli and joined the Italian Socialist Party in late 1913. Poor health, money problems and political commitment led him to give up his studies in early 1915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The failure of the workers' councils to grow into a national movement convinced Gramsci that a Communist party in the Leninist sense was needed. The group around L'Ordine Nuovo allied with Bordiga's far larger abstentionist faction, and on 21 January 1921 the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a>. Gramsci was a leader from the start but subordinate to Bordiga until 1924. In 1922 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as the party's representative, where he met the violinist Julia Schucht; they married in 1923.</p><p>On 9 November 1926 the Fascist government passed emergency laws after an alleged attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s life, and police arrested Gramsci despite his parliamentary immunity. His prosecutor said: &quot;For twenty years we must stop this brain from functioning.&quot; He was sentenced to five years on the island of Ustica and then, the following year, to twenty years in Turi. His health failed in prison, and a campaign organised by Piero Sraffa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> demanded his release. He was moved to a clinic at Formia in 1933 and to one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> two years later, and was too ill to travel when his sentence ended in April 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Gramsci filled more than 30 notebooks in prison, covering Italian history, fascism, Taylorism and Fordism, the state, religion and popular culture. His best-known concept, cultural hegemony, holds that the capitalist class maintains power by force and by spreading its own values until they pass for common sense. He divided the state into political society, which rules by force, and civil society, where leadership rests on consent. He also rejected economic determinism and the idea that revolution follows automatically from historical laws, and argued that each class produces its own organic intellectuals. Critics charge that his approach treats ideas as a struggle for power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonio Gramsci” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gaetano Salvemini held that a lack of understanding and action by politicians let corruption and organised crime flourish in the south, and <b>Antonio Gramsci</b> criticised the limited presence of the masses in politics and the lack of land reform.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Antonio Gramsci</b> explained capitalism’s survival by its use of civil society institutions to shape public ideology.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ales, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.77,8.82&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Ales on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Antonio Gramsci"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5577 4147h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5577 4147h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.77,8.82&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
